# 785. Is Graph Bipartite?

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite/
- **Topic:** Graph, DFS, Graph Coloring, Bipartite Graph
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

We need to divide the graph nodes into two different groups such that no two adjacent nodes belong to the same group.

I thought of maintaining a visited array that stores the color assigned to each node.

We can use two colors, 0 and 1, and perform DFS to assign opposite colors to adjacent nodes.

## 2. My Approach

1. Initialize a visited array with -1, representing unvisited nodes.
2. Start DFS from every unvisited node because the graph may be disconnected.
3. Assign color 0 to the starting node.
4. For every adjacent node:
   - If it is unvisited, assign the opposite color.
   - If it already has the same color as the current node, return false.
5. If all components are successfully colored, return true.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

The main thing to remember is that the graph may be disconnected.

Therefore, starting DFS only from node 0 is insufficient.

We must iterate through all nodes and start DFS whenever we find an unvisited node.

## 4. What Clicked

The important condition is:

vis[curr] = 1 - vis[node];

This automatically switches between the two colors.

- If the current color is 0, the next color becomes 1.
- If the current color is 1, the next color becomes 0.

If two adjacent nodes have the same color, the graph is not bipartite.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

Reference implementation:

class Solution {
    public boolean isBipartite(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] vis = new int[n];

        Arrays.fill(vis, -1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (vis[i] == -1) {
                vis[i] = 0;

                if (!dfs(graph, i, vis)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    boolean dfs(int[][] graph, int node, int[] vis) {
        for (int curr : graph[node]) {
            if (vis[curr] == -1) {
                vis[curr] = 1 - vis[node];

                if (!dfs(graph, curr, vis)) {
                    return false;
                }
            } else if (vis[curr] == vis[node]) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(V + E)
- Space Complexity: O(V), including the visited array and recursion stack.

## 7. Revision Notes

- A bipartite graph can be colored using two colors.
- Every edge must connect nodes with different colors.
- Same-colored adjacent nodes indicate that the graph is not bipartite.
- Always handle disconnected components.
- The same approach works with DFS and BFS.
